From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 05:30:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 031/184] segacd: let the drive pick the way to talk to it

Four discs, four guesses of mine about how a command reaches the CDD,
four wrong. Spending somebody else's CD-Rs one hypothesis at a time is
the wrong shape for this problem, so the guessing moves into the
firmware.

It carries six ways of sending: the ten nibbles as five words or as
ten bytes, crossed with what happens to HOCK afterwards -- nothing,
set it, or take it low and back up, which is the edge the last commit
claimed to produce and did not, since writing a set bit is not an
edge. Each gets eight attempts before the next one's turn.

The drive is the judge. A strategy counts as working only when the
reply names the sub-command it was asked for, and only after doing so
for two different sub-commands, since a single match could be a value
already sitting in the register. The winner is latched and the normal
seven-question sweep resumes on top of it.

Six failures would be worth as much as any success: that rules out the
trigger being a write performed in the wrong order or the wrong width,
which is the entire family of theories tried so far.

Verified with the answer detection forced off, so the search really
does cycle 0-5 and wrap, on both cores, without the link score
dropping or the machine hanging -- including the two strategies that
take HOCK low, which are the only ones that could plausibly end a
working link.
